To prevent data leakage when preparing datasets in JavaScript, always split your raw data into training and test sets before calculating any summary statistics or applying transformations . Unlike Python ecosystems that offer built-in pipeline wrappers (like scikit-learn ), JavaScript requires you to explicitly separate your "fit" (learning parameters) and "transform" (applying parameters) logic so test data never influences training.

1. Core Rules to Prevent Leakage

Split before preprocessing. Never compute dataset-wide averages, standard deviations, or vocabulary mappings before dividing your array into training and test splits.
Fit only on training data. Calculate scaling bounds, imputation values, and categorical encodings strictly using your train array.
Apply saved parameters to test data. Reuse the exact mean, min/max, or lookup tables derived from the training set to transform your test array.
Respect time order for sequential data. Use chronological slicing (slice ) instead of random shuffling when working with time-series or user-event logs so future events do not leak into past training examples.
Audit features for target leakage. Remove any columns recorded after the prediction event occurs, such as a refundDate or cancellationReason when predicting churn.

2. Clean JavaScript Implementation Pattern
Because standard JavaScript libraries like TensorFlow.jsTensorFlow.js or ml.js often leave tabular preprocessing to the developer, creating a reusable Scaler class enforces strict separation between fitting and transforming.

javascript
// 1. Split raw data BEFORE computing any statistics
function trainTestSplit(data, testRatio = 0.2) {
  // Use chronological slicing instead of shuffling for time-series data
  const shuffled = [...data].sort(() => Math.random() - 0.5);
  const splitIndex = Math.floor(data.length * (1 - testRatio));

  return {
    train: shuffled.slice(0, splitIndex),
    test: shuffled.slice(splitIndex)
  };
}

// 2. Encapsulate fit/transform logic to prevent leakage
class StandardScaler {
  constructor() {
    this.mean = 0;
    this.std = 1;
  }

  // Learn parameters ONLY from training data
  fit(trainValues) {
    const n = trainValues.length;
    this.mean = trainValues.reduce((sum, val) => sum + val, 0) / n;
    const variance = trainValues.reduce((sum, val) => sum + (val - this.mean)  2, 0) / n;
    this.std = Math.sqrt(variance) || 1; // Prevent division by zero
  }

  // Apply stored training parameters to any dataset
  transform(values) {
    return values.map(val => (val - this.mean) / this.std);
  }
}

// Example Workflow
const rawData = [12, 15, 14, 10, 85, 13, 16, 11, 90, 14];
const { train, test } = trainTestSplit(rawData, 0.2);

const scaler = new StandardScaler();
scaler.fit(train); // Fits strictly on train

const X_train_scaled = scaler.transform(train);
const X_test_scaled = scaler.transform(test); // Uses train's mean and std


3. Handling Edge Cases in JavaScript

Unseen categories in test data. When building one-hot or label encoders on train , always include an "UNKNOWN" fallback bucket so unseen strings in test do not throw errors or require refitting.
Cross-validation loops. If you implement K-fold cross-validation in JavaScript, instantiate and call .fit() inside every fold loop rather than once outside the loop.
Object mutation. Deep-clone records (structuredClone ) before preprocessing if your transformation functions mutate objects in place across splits.

